<title>GERMANY: German insurers 1996 premiums inch higher.</title>
<headline>German insurers 1996 premiums inch higher.</headline>
<dateline>REDEBEUL, Germany 1997-03-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>Calendar 1996</p>
<p>(in millions of marks unless otherwise stated)</p>
<p>    Premium income			     232,200 up 2.8 pct</p>
<p>Of which</p>
<p>    Life insurance			     92,800  vs 88,400</p>
<p>    Private health insurance		 33,900  vs 32,100</p>
<p>    Non-life insurance			 96,000  vs 96,000</p>
<p>	Motor insurance			  42,500  vs 44,100</p>
<p>	Third-party liabilty		   11,000  vs 10,700</p>
<p>	Private accident			  9,400  vs  9,100</p>
<p>	Fire					  6,500  vs  6,600</p>
<p>	Housing				     6,400  vs  6,000</p>
<p>	Legal costs				 4,600  vs  4,300</p>
<p>	Home contents			     4,500  vs  4,300</p>
<p>	Transport				   3,100  vs  3,100</p>
<p>	Credit, air and nuclear		 2,400  vs  2,200</p>
<p>NOTE - The data were released by the German Insurance Association (GDV) and refer to the whole German insurance industry. All data are preliminary. The overall increase in premium income was the slowest in the post-war era, the GDV said, and was due to the weak domestic economy. Sales were likely to remain stagnant in the current year.</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
</text>
